    {
      "repository": "martin-olivier/airgorah",
      "url": "https://github.com/martin-olivier/airgorah",
      "trending_rank": 15,
      "description": "A Wi-Fi security auditing app for Linux, written in Rust with a GTK4 interface: it captures nearby traffic, finds clients on access points, captures handshakes and PMKIDs, and can deauthenticate clients and crack access-point passwords.",
      "why_interesting": "It puts the usual command-line Wi-Fi audit workflow ==behind a desktop interface==, and runs the GUI as a normal user, raising privileges only for a small agent through polkit. It has been maintained since 2022; version 0.9.0 came out in September.",
      "caveat": "Deauthentication and password cracking are attacks: ==use it only on networks you own== or are authorized to test, as its own README warns. It runs on Linux only and needs a wireless card that supports monitor mode and packet injection.",
